Jim Thomas has send me a copy of the U. of Oregon policy. I'm happy to
report that it does *not* authorize suspensionless searches. (It does,
however, confuse copyright and license. More of the SPA's work?)

The University of Oregon's "Declaration" of recognition of the UO
software policy reads:

==================================================
I, ___________, declare that I have the use of a personal
computer (UO Inventory No._____). I further declare that I am
aware that making copies of or using applications programs or
operating systems without an appropriate license (i.e. by
serial number, if any, and version designation) is--except for
public domain software and shareware--a violation of copyright
law. I understand further that if I _knowingly_ make copies of or
use unauthorized copies of such commercial software, I may be
personally liable and the University, the State of Oregon, and
the State Liability Fund will very probably not defend or
indemnify me for such liability. Finally, I declare that, to the
best of my knowledge (aided by hard-disk directory reviews which I
will perform no less often than every six months), I have no
unauthorized copies of software on my hard disk or on the
diskettes in my possession or to which I have access. I understand
that the person or office which obtained the license is
responsible to provide photocopies of the license to other users
in multi-user arrangements. Thus, I agree to do so if I obtain
multi-user software. I also agree to make reasonable efforts to
get a copy of the license when I merely operate multi-user
software obtained by others.

       DATED: ___________, 19___.

             Signed: ________________________________________
             Typed name of employee:_________________________
==================================================================
